 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Axis Alignment:** Standard cross-sectional images can only be made across the x, y, and z axes. If the data is not aligned well with these axes, the result may be poor unless the Data Rotate Filter is used [S1].
-*   **Preview vs. Render:** The filtering operations (including clipping and transparency) are applied in the preview environment. They do not automatically persist to the final PovRay rendering unless the data is exported [S1].
+*   **Axis Alignment:** Standard cross-sectional images can only be made across the x, y, and z axes. If the data is not aligned well with these axes, the result may be poor unless the Data Rotate Filter is used [S4].
+*   **Preview vs. Render:** The filtering operations (including clipping and transparency) are applied in the preview environment. They do not automatically persist to the final PovRay rendering unless the data is exported [S4].
+*   **Large Volumes:** Implementation for very large data volumes (hundreds of gigabytes) can become complex to manage [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### Three-faced Image Filter
-Displays three cross-sections simultaneously, typically corresponding to the three principal axes (x, y, z) [S1]. It includes an **Offset** parameter, which defines the distance between the projected image and the volume [S1]. This allows the user to view the three orthogonal slices with a spatial gap, providing context of their position within the volume [S1].
+### [Three-faced Image Filter](15359-three-faced-image-filter.md)
+Displays three cross-sections simultaneously. It includes an **Offset** parameter, which defines the distance between the projected image and the volume. This allows the user to view the three orthogonal slices with a spatial gap, providing context of their position within the volume.
 
 ### Data Rotate Filter
-Rotates the data itself within the volume, rather than just rotating the databox or the view [S1]. This is necessary because the standard Cross Section Filter can only generate cross-sectional images across the x, y, and z axes [S1]. If the volume data is misaligned, this filter reorients the data to allow for proper reslicing [S1]. **Pitfall:** This filter works only in the preview environment. Filtered data must be exported to new `.df3` files to be used in PovRay rendering [S1].
+Rotates the data itself within the volume, rather than just rotating the databox or the view [S4]. This is necessary because the standard Cross Section Filter can only generate cross-sectional images across the x, y, and z axes [S4]. If the volume data is misaligned, this filter reorients the data to allow for proper reslicing [S4]. **Pitfall:** This filter works only in the preview environment. Filtered data must be exported to new `.df3` files to be used in PovRay rendering [S4].
+
+### Isopycnic Layer Slicing
+A feature allowing users to "dive" down to any isopycnic layer and examine slices of any dimensions [S7, unofficial]. Users can select specific layers (e.g., layers 2 and 13) and examine slices in various orientations (XZ, YZ) using transfer functions to build custom color maps [S7, unofficial].
 
